Z||000004||Will||05-24-2001||07:56 PM||||The Northstar crankshaft position sensor pulses 32x per crank revolution. The ICM converts this to a 24x signal.<P>The Caprice uses either an L99 or an LT1 engine, both of which use LT1 architecture.<BR>The optical pickup in the LT1 distributor has a slot for every degree of camshaft rotation, which is every 2 degrees of crankshaft rotation, or a 180x signal; not readily compatible with a 24x.<BR>Also, one would have to find a way of mounting an LT1 distributor on the Northstar; possibly by drilling a hole in one of the cam covers and driving it directly off one of the cams. My father and I have discussed this; while it is a possibility, it is a possibility I'm saving until everything DIS fails.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||05-24-2001||08:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>The Chip in a caprice is based on a 486 processor , and does not monitor nearly the number of sensors the Northstar does.<P>I heard this from a friend who runs the one lap of america<P>The internals of a Northstar computer are Motorola Based if I remember correctly.<P><BR>I think you would cause more problems than you would solve. There are a few people out there working on this problem. Chris Moore is Working With Lingenfelter Performance enginnering on solving the problem. If anyone figures it out , it will be them.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The '94+ LT1 electronics are almost as sophisticated as the N* electronics. The LT1 only lacks individual cylinder knock retard and camel mode (no coolant). Well... OK, it doesn't have post-cat O2 sensors, but I'm not really worried about those.<BR>All the other capabilites--dual O2 sensors, dual knock sensors, oil level, MAF, MAP, etc.--are the same.<P><BR>&gt;'96 N* electronics are by Siemens.<BR>&lt;'95 N* electronics are Delco, and probably use a Motorola processor.<BR>I don't know what architecture the LT1 uses right off, but it's almost certainly NOT x86 based.<BR>I do know that the LT1 optical distributor pickup is manufactured by Mitsubishi. So much for domestic hardware.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
